WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.966 --> 00:00:17.534
OK.

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:20.270
Let's get started.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.805
How's everyone doing?

00:00:21.805 --> 00:00:22.672
Wonderful.

00:00:22.672 --> 00:00:24.607
Come on, let's do
better than that.

00:00:24.607 --> 00:00:26.309
How is everyone doing?

00:00:26.309 --> 00:00:27.077
Wonderful!

00:00:27.077 --> 00:00:28.345
Thank you.

00:00:28.345 --> 00:00:31.481
Welcome to 3.091.

00:00:31.481 --> 00:00:33.717
This is the first day.

00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:35.385
And I'm really excited.

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:37.253
I hope you are too.

00:00:37.253 --> 00:00:39.456
In this very first
lecture, we're

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:42.425
going to do a little
bit of administration,

00:00:42.425 --> 00:00:46.229
tell you a little bit about
the class and how it's set up.

00:00:46.229 --> 00:00:47.964
And then we'll give
a little mini--

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:50.734
we'll have time for a mini
lecture in the second part

00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:52.102
of the lecture today.

00:00:52.102 --> 00:00:54.938
So I thought I'd
start by inducing me.

00:00:54.938 --> 00:00:57.007
My name is Jeff Grossman.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:00:59.809
I am in Course Three, that's the
Department of Material Science

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:01.411
and Engineering.

00:01:01.411 --> 00:01:04.447
My background, I went
to Hopkins, undergrad,

00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:08.718
moved my way towards the
west coast, Illinois PhD.

00:01:08.718 --> 00:01:11.488
And I did a post-doc
at Berkeley.

00:01:11.488 --> 00:01:14.190
And then I came here to
MIT about nine years ago

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.493
when I joined the faculty.

00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:19.929
My own passion, my own
interest in research

00:01:19.929 --> 00:01:26.269
is in materials for energy and
water and chemical processes

00:01:26.269 --> 00:01:27.370
and separations.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.672
So I thought I'd just give
you a little example of what

00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:30.373
I mean by that.

00:01:30.373 --> 00:01:32.741
So I get really excited
about the ability

00:01:32.741 --> 00:01:35.712
to take the material
and make it do something

00:01:35.712 --> 00:01:37.580
that it can't do
now, but that it

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.408
needs to do to solve a problem.

00:01:39.408 --> 00:01:40.616
Maybe it needs to be cheaper.

00:01:40.616 --> 00:01:43.652
Maybe it needs to
be more efficient.

00:01:43.652 --> 00:01:46.256
So one example is
in this material.

00:01:46.256 --> 00:01:49.125
This is a barrel of oil.

00:01:49.125 --> 00:01:51.694
And what we do today
with this barrel of oil

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:53.596
is we mostly burn it.

00:01:53.596 --> 00:01:54.898
A lot of what we do is burn it.

00:01:54.898 --> 00:01:56.648
Now if you do that,
and you think about it

00:01:56.648 --> 00:02:01.471
in terms of energy, that's
159 liters of this material.

00:02:01.471 --> 00:02:02.906
And if you think
about what you're

00:02:02.906 --> 00:02:05.975
carrying about as energy,
you get 1.73 megawatt hours

00:02:05.975 --> 00:02:08.272
of energy out of that.

00:02:08.272 --> 00:02:10.814
But see, I like to think about
materials, again, and ask them

00:02:10.814 --> 00:02:14.684
what they can do differently
for me, for these problems.

00:02:14.684 --> 00:02:16.486
And so if you think
about that and you say,

00:02:16.486 --> 00:02:18.688
well, OK, let's just
take 1% of the carbon

00:02:18.688 --> 00:02:21.157
in that barrel of oil
and do something with it.

00:02:21.157 --> 00:02:23.793
Let's make thin
film solar cells.

00:02:23.793 --> 00:02:25.829
Well, if you do that and
they're not very good,

00:02:25.829 --> 00:02:28.531
they're 5% efficient, they
completely die in a year,

00:02:28.531 --> 00:02:31.634
you get 10,000 times as
much energy over that year

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:32.402
than burning it.

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:33.837
So it's an example
of what you can

00:02:33.837 --> 00:02:36.805
do when you think about
using elements differently.

00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:39.709
That's what I get really
excited about in my research.

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:41.711
And that's just one example.

00:02:41.711 --> 00:02:45.482
You can take that
same carbon and you

00:02:45.482 --> 00:02:48.118
can make a thermoelectric,
that's on top.

00:02:48.118 --> 00:02:50.820
Or you can make the
world's thinnest filter.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.089
That's a piece of graphene
with a hole in it.

00:02:53.089 --> 00:02:56.559
And that's still working with
just one element, carbon.

00:02:56.559 --> 00:02:57.411
All right?

00:02:57.411 --> 00:02:58.828
So there are many,
many things you

00:02:58.828 --> 00:03:02.298
can do once you understand
what these elements are

00:03:02.298 --> 00:03:03.733
and how they combine together.

00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:05.201
And that's what
we're going to talk

00:03:05.201 --> 00:03:07.437
about in this class
over the fall.

00:03:07.437 --> 00:03:08.938
How many elements
are in your phone?

00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:10.807
Listen, we just talked
about one element.

00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:13.843
Anybody know how many
elements are in your phone?

00:03:13.843 --> 00:03:14.344
How many?

00:03:14.344 --> 00:03:15.812
Take a guess.

00:03:15.812 --> 00:03:17.380
Seven.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.315
27.

00:03:19.315 --> 00:03:20.617
OK, it depends.

00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:21.818
It depends.

00:03:21.818 --> 00:03:24.954
Do you have the Samsung?

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:27.423
If you've got the
Samsung whatever,

00:03:27.423 --> 00:03:28.591
it might light on fire.

00:03:28.591 --> 00:03:30.560
If you've got the
newer one, it won't.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:31.561
Do you have an iPhone?

00:03:31.561 --> 00:03:32.362
Well you know what?

00:03:32.362 --> 00:03:34.364
In the iPhone, there's 63.

00:03:34.364 --> 00:03:39.402
63 elements.

00:03:39.402 --> 00:03:41.437
So we have these
lectures in Course Three,

00:03:41.437 --> 00:03:42.772
they're called the wolf lecture.

00:03:42.772 --> 00:03:44.474
And I gave one a
couple of years ago.

00:03:44.474 --> 00:03:45.708
If you're interested in
hearing a little bit more

00:03:45.708 --> 00:03:47.710
about what I do or what
some other people do who

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:49.164
think about materials
in this way,

00:03:49.164 --> 00:03:50.914
you can use Google
wolf lecture and you'll

00:03:50.914 --> 00:03:53.249
get to some of the videos.

00:03:53.249 --> 00:03:55.952
And I'll mention it when they
come around during the year.

00:03:55.952 --> 00:04:00.182
OK, so that's a
little intro to me.

00:04:00.182 --> 00:04:01.057
What about the class?

00:04:01.057 --> 00:04:03.626
So as I said, the class--
at the heart of the class

00:04:03.626 --> 00:04:07.096
is kind of what
I just described.

00:04:07.096 --> 00:04:11.634
But in order to do that, we need
to know some very basic things.

00:04:11.634 --> 00:04:17.406
We need to know how
atoms are arranged,

00:04:17.406 --> 00:04:20.136
the atomic arrangement
right there.

00:04:20.136 --> 00:04:22.011
But we also need to know
what atoms are there

00:04:22.011 --> 00:04:22.845
in the first place.

00:04:22.845 --> 00:04:27.050
So if we know that, if
we know those things,

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.086
then we can build,
then we can build.

00:04:30.086 --> 00:04:31.321
We can get properties.

00:04:31.321 --> 00:04:32.722
We can get structure.

00:04:32.722 --> 00:04:34.357
We can get processing.

00:04:34.357 --> 00:04:35.425
We can get performance.

00:04:35.425 --> 00:04:37.619
And we can talk about
how all of these things

00:04:37.619 --> 00:04:38.494
relate to each other.

00:04:38.494 --> 00:04:39.929
They're all correlated.

00:04:39.929 --> 00:04:41.698
If I change the
processing of something,

00:04:41.698 --> 00:04:43.800
I change the properties.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.568
How do you know how
it changes when you've

00:04:45.568 --> 00:04:46.836
got to know these things?

00:04:46.836 --> 00:04:48.972
And at the core of
it all is really

00:04:48.972 --> 00:04:53.276
the thesis of this
class, which is

00:04:53.276 --> 00:04:58.414
that the electronic structure--

00:04:58.414 --> 00:05:02.047
Paul will be talking
about this-- structure

00:05:02.047 --> 00:05:15.365
of the elements holds
the key to understanding.

00:05:15.365 --> 00:05:20.436
That really is what
this class is about.

00:05:20.436 --> 00:05:21.337
Holds the key.

00:05:21.337 --> 00:05:24.641
The electronic structure of
the elements holds the key.

00:05:24.641 --> 00:05:26.542
That's what we're
here to talk about.

00:05:26.542 --> 00:05:30.013
And the first part of this class
is really the basic foundations

00:05:30.013 --> 00:05:32.282
of chemistry.

00:05:32.282 --> 00:05:34.217
Some of you may have
seen some of this before.

00:05:34.217 --> 00:05:35.718
We're going to build
these elements.

00:05:35.718 --> 00:05:36.586
We're going to talk about them.

00:05:36.586 --> 00:05:38.254
We're going to learn
about the electrons

00:05:38.254 --> 00:05:40.123
and the electronic structure.

00:05:40.123 --> 00:05:43.035
But then we're going to
make solids out of them

00:05:43.035 --> 00:05:44.827
and we're going to talk
about what those do

00:05:44.827 --> 00:05:47.597
and how the chemistry
relates to the solids

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:50.800
and to the properties
of those solids.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.335
Now, I want you to
know, again, this

00:05:52.335 --> 00:05:54.404
is the sort of
administrative introduction.

00:05:54.404 --> 00:05:56.039
You are not alone.

00:05:56.039 --> 00:06:01.577
On this journey, you have
many, many resources.

00:06:01.577 --> 00:06:06.649
And we are here to help you,
to help you learn this material

00:06:06.649 --> 00:06:09.352
and do the best you can.

00:06:09.352 --> 00:06:11.287
So first you have me.

00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:12.488
You have your TA.

00:06:12.488 --> 00:06:13.956
We'll talk about
them in a second.

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:15.391
You have Laura.

00:06:15.391 --> 00:06:17.393
Oh, we're going to talk
about Laura in a second.

00:06:17.393 --> 00:06:19.529
You have the textbook
and the internet.

00:06:19.529 --> 00:06:22.700
I hear there's stuff
on the internet.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:23.700
And you have each other.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.036
And I really want
you to work together.

00:06:27.036 --> 00:06:27.704
Thank you.

00:06:27.704 --> 00:06:28.871
Who said --that was...

00:06:28.871 --> 00:06:31.474
I was like, that hit made right
here, right in the first 10

00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:33.609
minutes of class.

00:06:33.609 --> 00:06:35.411
So the textbook is Averill.

00:06:35.411 --> 00:06:38.047
It's a really good textbook.

00:06:38.047 --> 00:06:39.215
It's a really good textbook.

00:06:39.215 --> 00:06:40.683
Please use it.

00:06:40.683 --> 00:06:41.884
Please.

00:06:41.884 --> 00:06:43.238
It's available here.

00:06:43.238 --> 00:06:45.321
Notes, like I said, will
be posted on the same day

00:06:45.321 --> 00:06:45.988
of each lecture.

00:06:45.988 --> 00:06:47.957
I'll post whatever you
see on this screen.

00:06:47.957 --> 00:06:49.625
I will post.

00:06:49.625 --> 00:06:50.860
All right, good.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.061
The goody bag.

00:06:52.061 --> 00:06:57.300
So the goody bag is another
part of your homework.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:01.504
And these will be
given out nine weeks.

00:07:01.504 --> 00:07:03.639
So sort of like the quizzes.

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:05.908
And in each goody
bag, which I'm going

00:07:05.908 --> 00:07:09.212
to talk about in a second,
there are things to do.

00:07:09.212 --> 00:07:15.551
This is a hands on compliment
to 309.1 lecturers and all

00:07:15.551 --> 00:07:18.020
the other materials.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:24.060
Now, these goody bags
are really important.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.229
And one of the two
quiz questions,

00:07:26.229 --> 00:07:30.566
every week that there's a
quiz, there are two questions.

00:07:30.566 --> 00:07:33.169
And one of them will be
directly related to something

00:07:33.169 --> 00:07:35.004
that you're supposed
to do in the goody bag.

00:07:35.004 --> 00:07:37.707
And in fact, we
will most times ask

00:07:37.707 --> 00:07:42.378
you to bring something into
the quiz that is in the bag.

00:07:42.378 --> 00:07:45.148
So don't throw it
away and please do it.

00:07:45.148 --> 00:07:46.949
Because one of the
two quiz questions

00:07:46.949 --> 00:07:48.751
will be very related
to the goody bag.

00:07:48.751 --> 00:07:52.388
And the other will be related
to problems, you know, lecture.

00:07:52.388 --> 00:07:55.057
Everything's related to lecture.

00:07:55.057 --> 00:07:57.370
But at least one will be
related to what's in the bag.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:58.995
So this, we really
take this seriously.

00:07:58.995 --> 00:08:02.231
This is a very important
part of your homework.

00:08:02.231 --> 00:08:03.533
And oh, there it is.

00:08:03.533 --> 00:08:05.401
Some of which you need
to bring to the quiz.

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:08.304
And we'll tell you what
that is before the quiz.

00:08:08.304 --> 00:08:11.073
I want to tell you a thing,
though, because here's

00:08:11.073 --> 00:08:15.462
the thing, goody bags are--
it's a little bit of what's

00:08:15.462 --> 00:08:16.045
going on here?

00:08:16.045 --> 00:08:17.046
This is a lecture class.

00:08:17.046 --> 00:08:21.417
Why are we getting goody bags?

00:08:21.417 --> 00:08:27.423
And it's because I believe
in the soul of MIT.

00:08:27.423 --> 00:08:29.659
I believe in the soul of MIT.

00:08:29.659 --> 00:08:35.231
And that is that we learn
best by thinking and doing,

00:08:35.231 --> 00:08:37.332
by thinking and doing.

00:08:37.332 --> 00:08:39.235
And even though this
is a lecture class,

00:08:39.235 --> 00:08:40.436
I still want you to do.

00:08:40.436 --> 00:08:42.738
I want you to have
stuff in your hands

00:08:42.738 --> 00:08:44.440
so that you can play
with the chemistry

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:48.711
that we're learning about.

00:08:48.711 --> 00:08:50.480
Now, this goes back.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:51.581
I said the soul of MIT.

00:08:51.581 --> 00:08:52.648
What do I mean?

00:08:52.648 --> 00:08:59.989
This goes back, all the way back
to before MIT was born, 1850s.

00:08:59.989 --> 00:09:02.285
You've got a group of
really smart people.

00:09:02.285 --> 00:09:04.160
They're getting together
and they're meeting.

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:05.628
And they're saying,
OK, we're going

00:09:05.628 --> 00:09:07.430
to start up a new university.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.665
What should we do?

00:09:09.665 --> 00:09:11.667
And they wrote out a plan.

00:09:11.667 --> 00:09:12.602
They wrote out a plan.

00:09:12.602 --> 00:09:14.060
And it's called
the institute plan.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:15.838
That makes sense, 1860.

00:09:15.838 --> 00:09:17.992
It's a great read.

00:09:17.992 --> 00:09:19.575
But I want to pull
out one part that's

00:09:19.575 --> 00:09:22.178
really important,
that what they wanted

00:09:22.178 --> 00:09:28.050
to do with this great new
institution was to do something

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:31.754
that would serve the interests
of the commerce and the arts,

00:09:31.754 --> 00:09:35.258
as well as of general
education, call for the most

00:09:35.258 --> 00:09:37.693
earnest cooperation
of intelligent culture

00:09:37.693 --> 00:09:40.429
with industrial pursuits,
intelligent culture,

00:09:40.429 --> 00:09:42.131
industrial pursuits.

00:09:42.131 --> 00:09:44.901
That is Mens et Manus.

00:09:44.901 --> 00:09:47.937
That is Mens et Manus.

00:09:47.937 --> 00:09:50.506
Mind and Hand.

00:09:50.506 --> 00:09:55.778
It's so important to MIT
that we put it on our logo.

00:09:55.778 --> 00:09:57.179
We put it on our logo.

00:09:57.179 --> 00:09:59.549
That's how important it is.

00:09:59.549 --> 00:10:04.787
We don't put some
animal on our logo.

00:10:04.787 --> 00:10:09.458
We put what matters,
mens et manus.

00:10:09.458 --> 00:10:14.430
We don't put the word truth.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:20.636
Veritas, veritas, I mean,
I'm not going to name names,

00:10:20.636 --> 00:10:22.905
Harvard.

00:10:22.905 --> 00:10:26.742
But I mean, isn't that
setting the bar kind of low?

00:10:26.742 --> 00:10:33.716
You know, were
they lying before?

00:10:33.716 --> 00:10:35.017
I don't know.

00:10:35.017 --> 00:10:38.487
I'm not-- look,
honestly I don't know.

00:10:38.487 --> 00:10:39.255
I don't know.

00:10:39.255 --> 00:10:43.159
But what I know is that what
we know is that, of course,

00:10:43.159 --> 00:10:44.193
the goal is truth.

00:10:44.193 --> 00:10:48.831
The difference is, we
know how to get it.

00:10:48.831 --> 00:10:51.968
Mens et manus is how to get it.

00:10:51.968 --> 00:10:54.303
Let me ask you a question.

00:10:54.303 --> 00:10:56.872
Why are you here?

00:10:56.872 --> 00:10:58.808
Not here in this classroom.

00:10:58.808 --> 00:11:01.310
I know you're in the classroom
because you signed up for it.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.279
Why are you at MIT?

00:11:03.279 --> 00:11:06.983
Why are you here at MIT?

00:11:06.983 --> 00:11:10.486
I can tell you, you
are here because you

00:11:10.486 --> 00:11:13.689
are some of the brightest, most
gifted, most talented students

00:11:13.689 --> 00:11:15.891
on the planet.

00:11:15.891 --> 00:11:16.392
Right?

00:11:16.392 --> 00:11:17.994
Thank you, whoever said that.

00:11:17.994 --> 00:11:19.629
Agreement.

00:11:19.629 --> 00:11:21.864
That was like a like online.

00:11:21.864 --> 00:11:24.033
But you are here
because you want

00:11:24.033 --> 00:11:28.537
to use those talents to make
the world a better place.

00:11:28.537 --> 00:11:34.543
You are here because you know
how to answer any question.

00:11:34.543 --> 00:11:37.747
But you are also here
because you are going

00:11:37.747 --> 00:11:39.915
to experience a transition.

00:11:39.915 --> 00:11:42.318
You are going to experience
a transition here.

00:11:42.318 --> 00:11:44.353
You are going to
make the transition

00:11:44.353 --> 00:11:47.623
from knowing how to answer
any question to knowing

00:11:47.623 --> 00:11:49.091
which question to ask.

00:11:49.091 --> 00:11:53.295
And that is the transition
from student to scholar.

00:11:53.295 --> 00:11:54.797
That is MIT.

00:11:54.797 --> 00:11:58.300
That's mens et manus.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:01.737
Now, it's not easy.

00:12:01.737 --> 00:12:06.842
You don't come here-- nobody
comes to MIT to phone it in.

00:12:06.842 --> 00:12:10.012
If universities
were restaurants,

00:12:10.012 --> 00:12:13.416
this wouldn't be that fancy one
where you go in and you order,

00:12:13.416 --> 00:12:15.551
and then somebody cooks
and brings you your food.

00:12:15.551 --> 00:12:18.054
This would be the one where we
all go back into the kitchen

00:12:18.054 --> 00:12:22.058
and together we make the
best meal we've ever had.

00:12:22.058 --> 00:12:23.859
That's the MIT way.

00:12:23.859 --> 00:12:25.428
That's the MIT way.

00:12:25.428 --> 00:12:31.121
And it's not about-- you don't
roam the halls here and bask

00:12:31.121 --> 00:12:34.637
in this reputation and think
of it as some privilege

00:12:34.637 --> 00:12:40.609
to be here, because you
are MIT's reputation.

00:12:40.609 --> 00:12:43.145
Freshmen, raise your hand.

00:12:43.145 --> 00:12:43.646
Yeah.

00:12:43.646 --> 00:12:46.482
Starting-- There's a few of you.

00:12:46.482 --> 00:12:51.087
Starting today, you
are MIT's reputation.

00:12:51.087 --> 00:12:53.589
Starting today.

00:12:53.589 --> 00:12:54.457
It's on you.

00:12:54.457 --> 00:13:00.963
So we don't walk around these
great halls and feel privilege.

00:13:00.963 --> 00:13:03.065
We feel responsibility.

00:13:03.065 --> 00:13:04.767
That's what it means to be here.

00:13:04.767 --> 00:13:08.237
OK?

00:13:08.237 --> 00:13:09.405
All right.

00:13:09.405 --> 00:13:10.272
Good.

00:13:10.272 --> 00:13:13.342
Well, now that we've got
that all straightened out,

00:13:13.342 --> 00:13:13.943
let's move on.

00:13:13.943 --> 00:13:18.581
That's the end of my
administrative stuff.

00:13:18.581 --> 00:13:23.452
And with the last
20, 25 minutes,

00:13:23.452 --> 00:13:29.058
I want to do a little bit of
an introduction to chemistry.

00:13:29.058 --> 00:13:38.300
And I'm not going to
ever test you on history.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:43.539
But I want to just kind of
get ourselves in the mood

00:13:43.539 --> 00:13:45.875
by going back.

00:13:45.875 --> 00:13:50.946
So I'll give you a little bit
of history here in a few slides.

00:13:50.946 --> 00:13:56.652
So we are interested in
solid state chemistry

00:13:56.652 --> 00:14:00.723
because chemistry is
that essential ingredient

00:14:00.723 --> 00:14:03.058
to understanding
the natural world.

00:14:03.058 --> 00:14:07.563
And the solid state is the link
between that and materials,

00:14:07.563 --> 00:14:09.765
and engineering.

00:14:09.765 --> 00:14:11.066
That is the link.

00:14:11.066 --> 00:14:14.236
And so we'll talk a lot
about this all the time.

00:14:14.236 --> 00:14:16.071
Oh oh.

00:14:16.071 --> 00:14:18.974
Come on out, Jerome.

00:14:18.974 --> 00:14:21.777
But where did chemistry begin?

00:14:21.777 --> 00:14:26.782
OK, now hold on.

00:14:26.782 --> 00:14:27.449
I've got a pen.

00:14:27.449 --> 00:14:28.350
Oh no, I got a--

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:29.685
OK, that's better.

00:14:29.685 --> 00:14:32.588
I thought it was a pen.

00:14:32.588 --> 00:14:34.190
Where did it begin?

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.358
Where did it begin?

00:14:36.358 --> 00:14:38.327
Well, people were mixing
stuff a long time ago.

00:14:38.327 --> 00:14:40.362
But really, and there's
some debate about this,

00:14:40.362 --> 00:14:42.832
you know, the word
chem itself, you know,

00:14:42.832 --> 00:14:44.733
it may have come from
chem, the land of chem,

00:14:44.733 --> 00:14:47.937
which means sort of
the rich soil in Egypt.

00:14:47.937 --> 00:14:50.172
Or maybe it came
from chemea in Greek,

00:14:50.172 --> 00:14:53.509
which meant sort of mixing
and pouring together.

00:14:53.509 --> 00:14:55.444
But the point is that
what they were doing was

00:14:55.444 --> 00:15:00.082
they were taking stuff and
making other stuff out of it.

00:15:00.082 --> 00:15:03.185
That dagger there is
from ancient Egypt.

00:15:03.185 --> 00:15:04.753
It's from around 3,000 BC.

00:15:04.753 --> 00:15:10.926
And the way they made that was
by taking stuff from a meteor.

00:15:10.926 --> 00:15:13.028
It was iron and some nickel
and some other things.

00:15:13.028 --> 00:15:14.230
But from a meteor.

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:17.299
And they were able to make a
really, really strong weapon

00:15:17.299 --> 00:15:18.534
out of it.

00:15:18.534 --> 00:15:21.270
They called those
daggers from heaven.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:23.505
But the point is
that what chemistry

00:15:23.505 --> 00:15:26.642
is about is it's about
how you mix these things

00:15:26.642 --> 00:15:29.144
and what are you mixing
in the first place.

00:15:29.144 --> 00:15:31.714
What was it that you
took out of the meteor?

00:15:31.714 --> 00:15:33.616
And why did it make
that instead of that?

00:15:33.616 --> 00:15:35.951
How did I get that dagger?

00:15:35.951 --> 00:15:37.753
That's really what
this is about.

00:15:37.753 --> 00:15:41.190
And so if we go back to
sort of the first people who

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.459
really started
discussing this, we

00:15:43.459 --> 00:15:46.295
start with Plato and Aristotle.

00:15:46.295 --> 00:15:54.003
And Plato had this idea,
probably some of you

00:15:54.003 --> 00:15:57.306
may have heard it, so
what is stuff made of?

00:15:57.306 --> 00:16:00.542
What is like the
essence of things?

00:16:00.542 --> 00:16:03.245
They really thought about
this and they debated it.

00:16:03.245 --> 00:16:08.751
And you know, Plato-- well,
Plato said there's four things.

00:16:08.751 --> 00:16:10.252
And some of you may
have heard this.

00:16:10.252 --> 00:16:12.855
There's earth.

00:16:12.855 --> 00:16:15.991
There's fire.

00:16:15.991 --> 00:16:18.227
There's air.

00:16:18.227 --> 00:16:23.832
And there's water.

00:16:23.832 --> 00:16:26.201
And everything is
made out of that.

00:16:26.201 --> 00:16:30.572
Now, you can see that's
a little limiting.

00:16:30.572 --> 00:16:32.474
Aristotle came along
and said, wait a second.

00:16:32.474 --> 00:16:33.369
Hold on.

00:16:33.369 --> 00:16:35.911
If I look out into the stars,
they don't seem to change much.

00:16:35.911 --> 00:16:38.047
So there must be something
else that's not quote,

00:16:38.047 --> 00:16:41.383
"earthly and corruptible."

00:16:41.383 --> 00:16:42.451
And he called that ether.

00:16:42.451 --> 00:16:46.188
But anyway, the point is,
it's hard to kind of explain

00:16:46.188 --> 00:16:47.256
everything with this.

00:16:47.256 --> 00:16:49.058
How are you going to
build a world out of--

00:16:49.058 --> 00:16:50.893
We can't even explain
the Boston weather

00:16:50.893 --> 00:16:54.630
with just these four words.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:55.798
So it was limiting.

00:16:55.798 --> 00:17:01.804
But then along came these
guys, Democritus and Leucippus,

00:17:01.804 --> 00:17:02.905
who was his teacher.

00:17:02.905 --> 00:17:07.376
And Democritus said, OK look,
there's something fundamental.

00:17:07.376 --> 00:17:12.982
So Democritus, he said, look I
believe there's something more

00:17:12.982 --> 00:17:15.416
than these four things.

00:17:15.416 --> 00:17:19.755
Democritus.

00:17:19.755 --> 00:17:22.891
And he said there are
these things called atoms.

00:17:22.891 --> 00:17:25.194
So he was an atomist.

00:17:25.194 --> 00:17:30.332
And atom is indivisible.

00:17:30.332 --> 00:17:32.468
Indivisible, that's the meaning.

00:17:32.468 --> 00:17:39.141
Atomist in Greek, it
meant indivisible, atom.

00:17:39.141 --> 00:17:40.876
And they fought about this.

00:17:40.876 --> 00:17:42.344
They fought about this a lot.

00:17:42.344 --> 00:17:45.881
And Plato, it's said, was
so upset about Democritus

00:17:45.881 --> 00:17:49.985
that he wanted all
of his books burned.

00:17:49.985 --> 00:17:53.822
That's a-- back in the day,
that was a serious diss.

00:17:53.822 --> 00:17:58.627
It would be like if I blocked
somebody on Instagram.

00:17:58.627 --> 00:17:59.261
Can I do that?

00:17:59.261 --> 00:17:59.928
Is that a thing?

00:17:59.928 --> 00:18:01.363
No, Snapchat.

00:18:01.363 --> 00:18:01.997
What?

00:18:01.997 --> 00:18:02.798
I don't know.

00:18:02.798 --> 00:18:03.332
I was right.

00:18:03.332 --> 00:18:04.433
See!

00:18:04.433 --> 00:18:07.136
That's how-- I'm blocking you.

00:18:07.136 --> 00:18:09.838
Burn your books.

00:18:09.838 --> 00:18:14.143
Seriously dissing each other.

00:18:14.143 --> 00:18:14.943
Democritus.

00:18:14.943 --> 00:18:19.048
Now, it really happened
quickly from there.

00:18:19.048 --> 00:18:24.520
A mere 2,000 years later
we get to modern chemistry.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:27.256
Why did it take 2,000 years?

00:18:27.256 --> 00:18:30.359
It took 2,000 years because
we were missing something.

00:18:30.359 --> 00:18:31.794
So we had a lot of alchemy.

00:18:31.794 --> 00:18:33.595
The thing about alchemy,
there are actually

00:18:33.595 --> 00:18:36.165
some really interesting
discoveries in alchemy

00:18:36.165 --> 00:18:39.902
but they always tied it to
something very non-rigorous,

00:18:39.902 --> 00:18:42.071
like oh, this works
because of the phases

00:18:42.071 --> 00:18:46.375
of the moon or the tides.

00:18:46.375 --> 00:18:50.279
And so it really needed
some rigorous way

00:18:50.279 --> 00:18:55.784
to study what things were made
of, what things were made of.

00:18:55.784 --> 00:19:01.857
And that came, oh, inevitable
that that might happen.

00:19:01.857 --> 00:19:02.591
Here we go.

00:19:02.591 --> 00:19:04.226
That came with the
scientific method

00:19:04.226 --> 00:19:08.764
in the 1600s and
Sir Francis Bacon.

00:19:08.764 --> 00:19:13.001
And I think a lot of you have
seen the scientific method.

00:19:13.001 --> 00:19:14.636
But it was pivotal
for chemistry.

00:19:14.636 --> 00:19:15.971
It was pivotal.

00:19:15.971 --> 00:19:17.606
Because it allowed
people to think

00:19:17.606 --> 00:19:19.241
about this question
of what stuff

00:19:19.241 --> 00:19:23.979
is made of but using
a rigorous approach.

00:19:23.979 --> 00:19:26.448
Making observation,
form a hypothesis,

00:19:26.448 --> 00:19:28.717
do an experiment, record.

00:19:28.717 --> 00:19:30.953
And that's what
people started doing.

00:19:30.953 --> 00:19:33.122
That is what, for
example, Robert Boyle

00:19:33.122 --> 00:19:36.458
did, one of the earlier
ones to think about things

00:19:36.458 --> 00:19:37.826
and talk about the element.

00:19:37.826 --> 00:19:40.095
They were all going
back to thinking

00:19:40.095 --> 00:19:44.733
about these same issues,
what are things made of?

00:19:44.733 --> 00:19:48.237
An element can't be broken
down into two or more

00:19:48.237 --> 00:19:51.173
simpler substances
by chemical means.

00:19:51.173 --> 00:19:52.074
So he was at that.

00:19:52.074 --> 00:19:54.109
He was going for that core.

00:19:54.109 --> 00:19:55.744
What is at the core?

00:19:55.744 --> 00:20:00.249
Oh, and then you had Priestly.

00:20:00.249 --> 00:20:06.421
Priestly discovered oxygen and
he did it by burning things.

00:20:06.421 --> 00:20:09.224
He did it by burning things.

00:20:09.224 --> 00:20:15.664
Just mentioning that word
makes me want to put those on.

00:20:15.664 --> 00:20:19.234
And so he really
studied combustion.

00:20:19.234 --> 00:20:22.137
He studied these reactions
that were happening with oxygen

00:20:22.137 --> 00:20:25.307
and carbon containing matter.

00:20:25.307 --> 00:20:27.009
It was combustion.

00:20:27.009 --> 00:20:27.809
So he burned stuff.

00:20:27.809 --> 00:20:28.710
He burned this.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:29.611
He burned that.

00:20:29.611 --> 00:20:30.245
He burned that.

00:20:30.245 --> 00:20:32.948
And he said, what
happens when I do that?

00:20:32.948 --> 00:20:34.016
That was another way.

00:20:34.016 --> 00:20:38.954
Priestly-- sorry, Boyle played
with pressure and volume.

00:20:38.954 --> 00:20:40.355
Pressure and volume
relationships

00:20:40.355 --> 00:20:42.558
for gases, that was
his way to try to get

00:20:42.558 --> 00:20:43.892
at what things are made of.

00:20:43.892 --> 00:20:47.563
Priestley wanted
to blow stuff up.

00:20:47.563 --> 00:20:51.526
Oh, and he also
worked with beer.

00:20:51.526 --> 00:20:52.734
He actually worked with beer.

00:20:52.734 --> 00:20:55.170
And he discovered
that the same gas that

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:57.739
comes out of fermenting
beer is the gas that

00:20:57.739 --> 00:21:00.008
comes out of combusting.

00:21:00.008 --> 00:21:03.278
So you got a guy who's
working on lighting stuff

00:21:03.278 --> 00:21:06.081
on fire and beer.

00:21:06.081 --> 00:21:08.750
And you can imagine
maybe that wasn't going

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:11.086
to be a good day at some point.

00:21:11.086 --> 00:21:13.789
And what happened is,
actually, his experiments did--

00:21:13.789 --> 00:21:17.559
this is true, they slowed down
when he fell into a vat of beer

00:21:17.559 --> 00:21:20.262
during one of his experiments.

00:21:20.262 --> 00:21:23.465
Now, the thing is, though,
that he studied combustion.

00:21:23.465 --> 00:21:26.501
And that makes me
think about combustion.

00:21:26.501 --> 00:21:32.541
And I feel like oh
oh, I feel like this

00:21:32.541 --> 00:21:41.083
is a good time for my goody bag,
which is to illustrate a point.

00:21:41.083 --> 00:21:47.055
Oh, thank you, Jerome.

00:21:47.055 --> 00:21:49.291
That's going to
some feed somewhere.

00:21:49.291 --> 00:21:54.196
OK, now the thing is that
when you go to a restaurant

00:21:54.196 --> 00:21:56.531
and they have real candles,
I get really happy about it.

00:21:56.531 --> 00:22:00.469
We'll tell candle stories
later in the term as well.

00:22:00.469 --> 00:22:02.371
And this is what you're doing.

00:22:02.371 --> 00:22:04.873
You are lighting
a candle on fire.

00:22:04.873 --> 00:22:08.243
Now, when you do that,
you see, here's the thing.

00:22:08.243 --> 00:22:11.079
You're actually-- I don't
want you to think about it

00:22:11.079 --> 00:22:12.414
as lighting a candle.

00:22:12.414 --> 00:22:14.983
I want you to now from
now on think about it

00:22:14.983 --> 00:22:16.704
as lighting C25H52.

00:22:16.704 --> 00:22:18.287
And in fact, if you
go to a restaurant

00:22:18.287 --> 00:22:20.188
and you want to ask them
if they have real candles,

00:22:20.188 --> 00:22:21.323
I don't care if you're
out with friends

00:22:21.323 --> 00:22:23.492
or maybe you're on a
date, raise your hand.

00:22:23.492 --> 00:22:27.596
Ask the waiter and say,
do you have any C25H52?

00:22:27.596 --> 00:22:31.566
And see if they know, see if
they've taken some chemistry.

00:22:31.566 --> 00:22:33.268
What you're doing is
you're doing that.

00:22:33.268 --> 00:22:35.904
You're combusting that fuel.

00:22:35.904 --> 00:22:39.574
See, the whole world runs
by lighting things on fire.

00:22:39.574 --> 00:22:41.743
You could light propane on fire.

00:22:41.743 --> 00:22:44.780
Or you could light
hydrogen on fire.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:49.051
That's where we said, yo, hands
on is a good way to learn.

00:22:49.051 --> 00:22:50.319
So let's see what that's like.

00:22:50.319 --> 00:22:52.220
There's the candle,
which is tilting.

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:54.356
I'm watching it.

00:22:54.356 --> 00:22:55.390
Really?

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:58.293
Oh, OK.

00:22:58.293 --> 00:23:00.228
Or you could light
hydrogen on fire.

00:23:00.228 --> 00:23:04.099
And if you do that,
this is what happens.

00:23:04.099 --> 00:23:06.001
So, OK, let's do that again.

00:23:06.001 --> 00:23:09.304
These bubbles aren't-- oh,
I should have kept this on.

00:23:09.304 --> 00:23:12.641
There we go.

00:23:12.641 --> 00:23:16.478
That's really, really fun.

00:23:16.478 --> 00:23:17.412
But I'm going to stop.

00:23:17.412 --> 00:23:19.356
Oh, I'm not going to
stop because you just

00:23:19.356 --> 00:23:20.148
turned it on again.

00:23:20.148 --> 00:23:23.518
Now you're coaxing
me to do more.

00:23:23.518 --> 00:23:24.820
Well, these are bigger bubbles.

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:25.903
These are bigger boulders.

00:23:25.903 --> 00:23:28.423
Let's see if this
gets a nice flame.

00:23:28.423 --> 00:23:30.625
Oh, thank you.

00:23:30.625 --> 00:23:32.961
There we go.

00:23:32.961 --> 00:23:40.035
OK, now that's my
goody bag for today.

00:23:40.035 --> 00:23:41.903
We run our world by
doing this, I mean,

00:23:41.903 --> 00:23:46.141
not by lighting hydrogen
bubbles on fire,

00:23:46.141 --> 00:23:49.411
but when you put your
phone in to charge it,

00:23:49.411 --> 00:23:50.412
you are lighting a fire.

00:23:50.412 --> 00:23:51.120
Think about that.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.915
You might not feel it.

00:23:53.915 --> 00:23:54.416
You are.

00:23:54.416 --> 00:23:56.251
No, I'm-- I love that reaction.

00:23:56.251 --> 00:23:57.319
I love that reaction.

00:23:57.319 --> 00:23:58.854
You are lighting a fire.

00:23:58.854 --> 00:24:01.723
It may not be you, but
somebody else's down the street

00:24:01.723 --> 00:24:03.058
at a power plant.

00:24:03.058 --> 00:24:05.227
This is how we run our
world, we burn things.

00:24:05.227 --> 00:24:09.030
And so this study of combustion
was extremely important.

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:13.402
And it's going to be
our first reaction.

00:24:13.402 --> 00:24:14.936
So what's happening?

00:24:14.936 --> 00:24:20.409
When you light C25H52 on
fire, what's happening

00:24:20.409 --> 00:24:21.943
is that you're reacting.

00:24:21.943 --> 00:24:25.387
You're doing--
remember, Priestley

00:24:25.387 --> 00:24:30.719
discovered H52 plus oxygen.
He discovered oxygen.

00:24:30.719 --> 00:24:35.257
And he also, remember,
this comes off of beer too.

00:24:35.257 --> 00:24:38.860
He also discovered these
other gases coming out.

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:41.029
Right?

00:24:41.029 --> 00:24:43.999
And that's the
chemical reaction.

00:24:43.999 --> 00:24:46.134
Is it the chemical reaction?

00:24:46.134 --> 00:24:49.070
It's not done yet.

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:50.238
It's not balanced.

00:24:50.238 --> 00:24:52.140
It's not balanced.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:54.242
Mass is being lost
left and right.

00:24:54.242 --> 00:24:56.211
Not OK.

00:24:56.211 --> 00:24:57.312
Not OK.

00:24:57.312 --> 00:25:00.382
So we must, when we
write down things

00:25:00.382 --> 00:25:04.986
that happen in chemistry like a
reaction, we must find balance.

00:25:04.986 --> 00:25:07.622
It's also important in life.

00:25:07.622 --> 00:25:09.524
But it's very
important in chemistry.

00:25:09.524 --> 00:25:13.795
And so if you balance this,
you're going to put a 2.

00:25:13.795 --> 00:25:18.533
Because here's the deal and I'll
talk about this more in a sec,

00:25:18.533 --> 00:25:29.077
C25H52 plus-- anybody
know how many O2s?

00:25:29.077 --> 00:25:32.180
38, whoever said
that, 38 is going

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:43.225
to go to 25CO2 plus 26H2O.

00:25:43.225 --> 00:25:46.027
These are the kinds of
things that are perfect,

00:25:46.027 --> 00:25:49.464
perfect to keep doing exercises
on though your problems,

00:25:49.464 --> 00:25:53.368
through your goody bags,
in your recitation.

00:25:53.368 --> 00:25:56.071
These are exactly the--
how did you do that?

00:25:56.071 --> 00:25:57.906
If you don't know,
you will soon.

00:25:57.906 --> 00:26:03.211
You'll get help, practice.

00:26:03.211 --> 00:26:04.746
I know, I know.

00:26:04.746 --> 00:26:07.148
I wrote this down.

00:26:07.148 --> 00:26:09.918
I'm just testing you.

00:26:09.918 --> 00:26:11.152
I made a mistake.

00:26:11.152 --> 00:26:16.558
Because the 2 was supposed
to go to the propane one

00:26:16.558 --> 00:26:19.127
because I wanted to write
that one and I got excited.

00:26:19.127 --> 00:26:22.597
And I went to that instead.

00:26:22.597 --> 00:26:24.599
And that would be 13O2.

00:26:24.599 --> 00:26:30.805
And that would be
8CO2, and so on, 10H2O.

00:26:30.805 --> 00:26:33.008
These are balanced.

00:26:33.008 --> 00:26:35.443
These are balanced.

00:26:35.443 --> 00:26:40.882
And balancing
reactions is important.

00:26:40.882 --> 00:26:41.383
Why?

00:26:41.383 --> 00:26:46.688
Because it balances the mass
and it tells us something else.

00:26:46.688 --> 00:26:49.090
Once we count atoms, you'll see.

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:52.694
We're going to count
atoms on Friday.

00:26:52.694 --> 00:26:56.531
But it's telling us that
you can't just lose stuff.

00:26:56.531 --> 00:26:57.732
You can't just lose stuff.

00:26:57.732 --> 00:26:58.800
You've got to have the--

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:02.737
Oh, and that, by
the way, is what--

00:27:02.737 --> 00:27:03.939
Lavoisier, Lavoisier.

00:27:03.939 --> 00:27:05.307
Where is Jerome?

00:27:05.307 --> 00:27:06.741
Oh, yeah.

00:27:06.741 --> 00:27:07.876
No.

00:27:07.876 --> 00:27:08.376
Shoot.

00:27:08.376 --> 00:27:10.312
Isn't that butane, not propane?

00:27:10.312 --> 00:27:11.279
Butane not propane.

00:27:11.279 --> 00:27:13.048
It very well might have been.

00:27:13.048 --> 00:27:15.050
C4 H10, yes.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:16.318
I believe that is butane.

00:27:16.318 --> 00:27:20.755
Thank you.

00:27:20.755 --> 00:27:22.557
Oh, Jerome, come back.

00:27:22.557 --> 00:27:25.226
Jerome helps the class
and he helps my French.

00:27:25.226 --> 00:27:26.661
Lavoisier?

00:27:26.661 --> 00:27:28.663
No.

00:27:28.663 --> 00:27:31.533
OK, say it.

00:27:31.533 --> 00:27:35.403
Lavoisier.

00:27:35.403 --> 00:27:37.238
OK, I tried.

00:27:37.238 --> 00:27:43.211
But now he said, look,
you've got to conserve mass.

00:27:43.211 --> 00:27:47.315
You can't lose-- you can't
create or destroy matter

00:27:47.315 --> 00:27:48.817
when you do chemistry.

00:27:48.817 --> 00:27:52.754
When you do this, when you
do this you cannot create

00:27:52.754 --> 00:27:56.691
or destroy matter.

00:27:56.691 --> 00:28:03.498
Conservation of mass, Lavoisier.

00:28:03.498 --> 00:28:06.767
It means that if I-- you
know, you get a little more

00:28:06.767 --> 00:28:10.805
than that, see
this is balancing.

00:28:10.805 --> 00:28:15.577
But you also can think about
whether you have anything--

00:28:15.577 --> 00:28:18.580
I probably should blow that out.

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:23.318
Whether you have anything-- did
you have too much, too little?

00:28:23.318 --> 00:28:24.519
Did I mix it just right?

00:28:24.519 --> 00:28:27.184
So like if you take-- let's
take another reaction just

00:28:27.184 --> 00:28:28.023
as an example.

00:28:28.023 --> 00:28:39.067
If I mix iron and oxygen to make
ferric oxide, oh, not balanced.

00:28:39.067 --> 00:28:43.438
Two, three, four.

00:28:43.438 --> 00:28:48.677
And I tell you, for example,
that I've got 10 grams of iron

00:28:48.677 --> 00:28:55.083
reacts with, let's see--

00:28:55.083 --> 00:28:57.085
OK, I'm going to give it
to you a different way.

00:28:57.085 --> 00:29:04.325
I'm going to say that it gives
you, reacts with O2 to give

00:29:04.325 --> 00:29:11.533
18.2 grams of FE203.

00:29:11.533 --> 00:29:14.903
This is an example
from the textbook.

00:29:14.903 --> 00:29:17.330
Then I know because
of conservation

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:25.776
of mass from Lavoisier that 8.2
grams-- if this reacts fully,

00:29:25.776 --> 00:29:30.485
right, if iron reacts
fully, it all goes away.

00:29:30.485 --> 00:29:31.886
There's none left.

00:29:31.886 --> 00:29:35.490
Then I must have, if I got
18.2 grams of ferric acid,

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:38.760
I must have reacted
8.2 grams of oxygen.

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:40.895
That's conservation of mass.

00:29:40.895 --> 00:29:44.032
But there's another thing
you can do with this.

00:29:44.032 --> 00:29:53.041
So I must have reacted
8.2 grams of oxygen.

00:29:53.041 --> 00:29:56.945
But there's another thing
because if I started, you know,

00:29:56.945 --> 00:30:09.324
if I started now with 10 grams
of O2 and 10 grams of iron,

00:30:09.324 --> 00:30:12.393
aha, I'm going to have excess.

00:30:12.393 --> 00:30:13.528
I know that.

00:30:13.528 --> 00:30:14.429
I know that now.

00:30:14.429 --> 00:30:15.497
I'm going to have excess.

00:30:15.497 --> 00:30:18.833
And the thing is that there's
something that's limiting here.

00:30:18.833 --> 00:30:21.936
What's in excess?

00:30:21.936 --> 00:30:25.073
The oxygen because I started
with the same amount of iron.

00:30:25.073 --> 00:30:27.375
There is more-- but
that means that we've

00:30:27.375 --> 00:30:33.506
got another term, which is that
iron is the limiting reagent.

00:30:33.506 --> 00:30:34.048
You see that?

00:30:34.048 --> 00:30:40.688
Because now I'm limited,
meaning this reaction

00:30:40.688 --> 00:30:43.825
goes and goes and goes and
something runs out first.

00:30:43.825 --> 00:30:47.328
That is the limiting reagent.

00:30:47.328 --> 00:30:51.633
And this was just by thinking
about Lavoisier's conservation

00:30:51.633 --> 00:30:52.934
of mass.

00:30:52.934 --> 00:30:55.937
I can't create or destroy
atoms in a reaction,

00:30:55.937 --> 00:30:57.238
not in this class.

00:30:57.238 --> 00:30:58.807
You can take nuclear
somewhere else.

00:30:58.807 --> 00:30:59.774
But not here.

00:30:59.774 --> 00:31:02.443
Here we don't destroy
or create matter.

00:31:02.443 --> 00:31:04.546
Limiting reagent,
balancing reactions,

00:31:04.546 --> 00:31:09.150
very, very fundamental
first chemical concepts.

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:12.687
Now, these guys were
playing around with stuff

00:31:12.687 --> 00:31:14.756
and really trying to
figure out, again,

00:31:14.756 --> 00:31:17.458
they're going back
to Democritus.

00:31:17.458 --> 00:31:22.674
What are these indivisible
elements, atoms, what are they?

00:31:22.674 --> 00:31:24.799
And all these guys were
starting to mess with that.

00:31:24.799 --> 00:31:26.661
Once they had the
scientific method,

00:31:26.661 --> 00:31:28.036
they were willing
to go very far.

00:31:28.036 --> 00:31:32.507
Here's Lavoisier's
list, 33 elements.

00:31:32.507 --> 00:31:35.143
And he tried to organize them.

00:31:35.143 --> 00:31:38.513
And in some cases, he
succeeded fairly well.

00:31:38.513 --> 00:31:39.113
Look at this.

00:31:39.113 --> 00:31:42.116
This is a, OK, where is Jerome?

00:31:42.116 --> 00:31:48.156
This is a Tableau
des Substances...

00:31:48.156 --> 00:31:50.658
No, not even close.

00:31:50.658 --> 00:31:53.695
But look, "Simples..."

00:31:53.695 --> 00:31:58.233
Simple, fundamental atoms.

00:31:58.233 --> 00:32:00.635
What are those things
that we're mixing together

00:32:00.635 --> 00:32:04.339
that we've been mixing together
for thousands of years?

00:32:04.339 --> 00:32:06.875
What is that thing I'm
pulling out of this oar

00:32:06.875 --> 00:32:08.710
and making stuff out of?

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:13.281
And he was trying to classify
them using these experiments.

00:32:13.281 --> 00:32:19.454
And some of these are actually
really good discoveries.

00:32:19.454 --> 00:32:21.456
I want you to experience this.

00:32:21.456 --> 00:32:23.725
I want you to go
back to this time.

00:32:23.725 --> 00:32:27.362
And that is what the
first goody bag is about.

00:32:27.362 --> 00:32:30.098
So what I have given
you in this goody bag

00:32:30.098 --> 00:32:36.237
is the most accurate measuring
device ever created and branded

00:32:36.237 --> 00:32:37.939
309.1.

00:32:37.939 --> 00:32:40.909
It's a ruler.

00:32:40.909 --> 00:32:42.310
I've given you
five metal strips.

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:45.013
And I want you to pretend that
you don't know what these are.

00:32:45.013 --> 00:32:47.649
How do you find out?

00:32:47.649 --> 00:32:52.987
You do nothing
with fire, nothing.

00:32:52.987 --> 00:32:56.157
But I gave you vinegar.

00:32:56.157 --> 00:32:58.059
And you do lots with vinegar.

00:32:58.059 --> 00:32:58.826
Pour it on them.

00:32:58.826 --> 00:32:59.928
Measure it, weigh it.

00:32:59.928 --> 00:33:01.529
Look at it.

00:33:01.529 --> 00:33:02.764
Shiny, not shiny.

00:33:02.764 --> 00:33:04.832
Different color, densities.

00:33:04.832 --> 00:33:08.636
Think about what
the differences are.

00:33:08.636 --> 00:33:10.338
And I want to put you
back in that time.

00:33:10.338 --> 00:33:12.373
And I want to make a
point here, goody bags

00:33:12.373 --> 00:33:14.442
are not just about
the questions I ask.

00:33:14.442 --> 00:33:18.212
I hope that you think
even beyond the Goody bag.

00:33:18.212 --> 00:33:21.282
So if you use that vinegar and
one of those things reacts,

00:33:21.282 --> 00:33:26.020
let's just suppose,
with the vinegar,

00:33:26.020 --> 00:33:28.723
think about what that is
and maybe vinegar is a test.

00:33:28.723 --> 00:33:30.658
Maybe it's a test
and you can react--

00:33:30.658 --> 00:33:33.962
you can pour vinegar and make
reactions with other things.

00:33:33.962 --> 00:33:36.264
Maybe you should be thinking
about where that thing is

00:33:36.264 --> 00:33:38.599
in the Infinite Corridor and
the whole Infinite Corridor

00:33:38.599 --> 00:33:40.301
should smell like vinegar.

00:33:40.301 --> 00:33:46.107
But we won't tell
President Reif about that.

00:33:46.107 --> 00:33:49.711
But that's what I mean, maybe
not the Infinite-- but explore.

00:33:49.711 --> 00:33:50.912
These are meant to be--

00:33:50.912 --> 00:33:54.115
I want you to really use
these as an adventure.

00:33:54.115 --> 00:33:56.718
You know, think outside the box.

00:33:56.718 --> 00:33:59.587
So you've got this most
accurate measuring device.

00:33:59.587 --> 00:34:01.189
You've got these
strips of pipette.

00:34:01.189 --> 00:34:03.458
And you've got some gloves.

00:34:03.458 --> 00:34:07.662
And that is your
first goody bag.

00:34:07.662 --> 00:34:11.232
OK, now though the
last thing that I'm

00:34:11.232 --> 00:34:13.400
going to tell you about
is why this matters.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:17.005
And just like the goody bags,
when I started teaching class

00:34:17.005 --> 00:34:21.409
three years ago, I
wanted to also protect

00:34:21.409 --> 00:34:24.846
a certain fundamental
part of each lecture.

00:34:24.846 --> 00:34:27.514
And I call it my why
this matters moment.

00:34:27.514 --> 00:34:30.784
Sometimes it goes on
for more than a moment.

00:34:30.784 --> 00:34:33.755
But I really, really
want every lecture

00:34:33.755 --> 00:34:38.226
to connect what we just
learned to a big picture.

00:34:38.226 --> 00:34:39.860
Most of the time,
it's some application

00:34:39.860 --> 00:34:42.463
or some global challenge.

00:34:42.463 --> 00:34:45.699
Right I want you to
see those connections,

00:34:45.699 --> 00:34:47.601
that what you're
learning is directly

00:34:47.601 --> 00:34:51.005
relevant to some big thing.

00:34:51.005 --> 00:34:53.875
So my why this matters
moment is really related

00:34:53.875 --> 00:34:56.777
to these discoveries themselves.

00:34:56.777 --> 00:34:58.579
Please give me till 11:55.

00:34:58.579 --> 00:35:00.709
I will always let you
go on time, 11:55.

00:35:00.709 --> 00:35:02.417
But please don't start
putting stuff away

00:35:02.417 --> 00:35:04.485
because it's distracting
to everybody.

00:35:04.485 --> 00:35:08.856
So two and a half minutes.

00:35:08.856 --> 00:35:13.594
We name the age we live in often
by the element, by the atom,

00:35:13.594 --> 00:35:17.498
by the material, by
the material that

00:35:17.498 --> 00:35:20.735
was most useful at the time.

00:35:20.735 --> 00:35:24.372
The Stone Age, the
Bronze Age, the Iron Age.

00:35:24.372 --> 00:35:26.607
I would say we've moved
through the industrial age,

00:35:26.607 --> 00:35:28.910
the age of plastics,
the age of silicon.

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:31.646
As a material scientist
and engineer, I love this.

00:35:31.646 --> 00:35:35.116
I love that you name the age
you live in by the material that

00:35:35.116 --> 00:35:36.284
mattered.

00:35:36.284 --> 00:35:41.155
But I also love that we will
never do that again, ever.

00:35:41.155 --> 00:35:44.225
And the reason is that we live
in a truly unique age now,

00:35:44.225 --> 00:35:47.962
a different age, one in
which we can put atoms,

00:35:47.962 --> 00:35:52.200
we can realize Feynman's dream
and put atoms anywhere we want.

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:56.504
The question is not,
can we make it as much

00:35:56.504 --> 00:35:59.240
any more as it is,
what should we make?

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:02.944
We live in the age
of atomic design.

00:36:02.944 --> 00:36:04.178
And that is really important.

00:36:04.178 --> 00:36:08.182
And I mentioned the phone
and the 63 elements.

00:36:08.182 --> 00:36:11.953
You know, look, this
is called a revolution.

00:36:11.953 --> 00:36:14.956
This is called a revolution.

00:36:14.956 --> 00:36:19.060
You went in 50 years
from $1 per transistor,

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:22.029
eight orders of
magnitude cheaper.

00:36:22.029 --> 00:36:26.534
In 2012, it became cheaper to
print a transistor on a chip

00:36:26.534 --> 00:36:29.804
than a character in a newspaper.

00:36:29.804 --> 00:36:31.739
That's a revolution
but that revolution,

00:36:31.739 --> 00:36:34.041
it started as a
processing revolution

00:36:34.041 --> 00:36:37.044
with one really important
element, silicon.

00:36:37.044 --> 00:36:41.849
And now it's a materials
revolution, with 63.

00:36:41.849 --> 00:36:44.352
It's a chemistry revolution.

00:36:44.352 --> 00:36:48.222
And the reason this matters so
much, what are these things,

00:36:48.222 --> 00:36:52.760
is because so many of
the problems that we face

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:56.664
in this world today, so many
of the global challenges,

00:36:56.664 --> 00:37:02.036
will rely on new chemistry
and on new materials.

00:37:02.036 --> 00:37:04.305
Those are the bottlenecks.

00:37:04.305 --> 00:37:06.407
Those are the bottlenecks
in costs, in efficiency,

00:37:06.407 --> 00:37:09.336
in processing, in properties.

00:37:09.336 --> 00:37:10.711
And those are the
kinds of things

00:37:10.711 --> 00:37:13.381
that we're going to be talking
about all throughout the fall.

00:37:13.381 --> 00:37:16.384
And that is our
construction set.

00:37:16.384 --> 00:37:19.820
And we will build
this on Friday.

00:37:19.820 --> 00:37:21.622
So see you guys all on Friday.

00:37:21.622 --> 00:37:23.589
